{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.6.6","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[],"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"source":"<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/code/rizviabiha/tools?scriptVersionId=366970788\" target=\"_blank\"><img align=\"left\" alt=\"Kaggle\" title=\"Open in Kaggle\" src=\"https://kaggle.com/static/images/open-in-kaggle.svg\"></a>","metadata":{},"cell_type":"markdown"},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Tools in LangChain\n\n## 1. What are Tools?\n\nModern LLMs have two important capabilities:\n\n1. **Reasoning:** They can understand a problem, analyse information, and decide what needs to be done.\n2. **Language Generation:** They can generate human-like text and communicate answers.\n\nHowever, an LLM cannot directly perform external actions just by generating text. For example, it cannot independently:\n- Call an external API.\n- Search the internet for real-time information.\n- Execute a Python function.\n- Access a database and modify its records.\n- Interact with external applications.\n\nTo perform these actions, we provide the LLM with **Tools**.\n\n**Definition:**\n\nA tool is a Python function or an API packaged in a way that an LLM can understand, select, and invoke when required.\n\nA tool has three important components:\n\n- **Name:** Identifies the tool.\n- **Description:** Explains what the tool does.\n- **Arguments:** Defines the inputs required by the tool.\n\nFor example, a multiplication tool might have:\n\n- Name: `multiply`\n- Description: `Multiply two numbers`\n- Arguments: `a` and `b`\n\nThe LLM can use this information to decide when and how to call the tool.\n\n## 2. How Tools Fit into the Agent Ecosystem\n\nAn AI agent is an LLM-powered system that can autonomously reason, make decisions, and take actions using external tools or APIs to achieve a goal.\n\nAn agent combines two major capabilities:\n\n| Component | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| LLM | Reasoning and decision-making |\n| Tools | Performing external actions |\n\n### How does it work?\n\nConsider this example:\n\n**User:** What is the latest IPL news?\n\n1. The user asks a question.\n2. The LLM understands that the question requires current information.\n3. The LLM decides to use an internet search tool.\n4. The agent invokes the search tool with the appropriate query.\n5. The search tool retrieves information from the internet.\n6. The retrieved results are provided back to the LLM.\n7. The LLM generates a response using those results.\n\n**Important:** The LLM decides which tool to use, but the actual tool execution is performed by the tool itself.\n\n## 3. Types of Tools in LangChain\n\nTools can broadly be classified into two categories:\n\n### A. Built-in Tools\n\nA built-in tool is a pre-built tool provided through LangChain integrations or its ecosystem.\n\nYou do not have to implement the underlying functionality yourself. You generally import the tool, configure it if required, and use it.\n\nExamples:\n\n**1. DuckDuckGo Search**\n\nUsed to search the internet.\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_community.tools import DuckDuckGoSearchRun\n\nsearch_tool = DuckDuckGoSearchRun()\n\nresults = search_tool.invoke(\"IPL news\")\n\nprint(results)\n```\n\n**2. Wikipedia Query**\n\nUsed to retrieve information from Wikipedia.\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_community.tools import WikipediaQueryRun\nfrom langchain_community.utilities import WikipediaAPIWrapper\n\nwiki_wrapper = WikipediaAPIWrapper()\n\nwiki_tool = WikipediaQueryRun(\n    api_wrapper=wiki_wrapper\n)\n\nresults = wiki_tool.invoke(\"Artificial Intelligence\")\n\nprint(results)\n```\n\n**3. Shell Tool**\n\nUsed to execute shell commands.\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_community.tools import ShellTool\n\nshell_tool = ShellTool()\n\nresults = shell_tool.invoke(\"whoami\")\n\nprint(results)\n```\n\nExample output:\n\n```text\nroot\n```\n\nHere, `whoami` is a shell command that displays the current user.\n\n**Security note:** Shell tools can execute potentially dangerous commands. Never give an agent unrestricted shell access, especially in production environments.\n\n### B. Custom Tools\n\nA custom tool is a tool that you create yourself.\n\nYou define its:\n- Name\n- Description\n- Input arguments\n- Functionality\n\n**When do we create custom tools?**\n\n1. When we want to call our own APIs.\n2. When we want to encapsulate our application's business logic.\n3. When we want an LLM to interact with our database, product, or application.\n4. When existing built-in tools do not provide the functionality we need.\n\nFor example, suppose we are building an e-commerce application. We can create custom tools for:\n\n- Checking product availability.\n- Fetching order details.\n- Cancelling an order.\n- Checking delivery status.\n\n## 4. Tools Are Also Runnables\n\nIn LangChain, tools follow the Runnable interface.\n\nThis means tools support common Runnable methods such as:\n\n| Method | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `invoke()` | Executes a tool with one input. |\n| `batch()` | Executes a tool on multiple inputs. |\n| `stream()` | Provides streaming output when supported. |\n\nExample:\n\n```python\nfrom langchain.tools import tool\n\n@tool\ndef multiply(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Multiply two numbers.\"\"\"\n    return a * b\n\nresult = multiply.invoke({\n    \"a\": 3,\n    \"b\": 5\n})\n\nprint(result)\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n15\n```\n\nHere, `invoke()` executes the tool with the provided arguments.\n\n## 5. Ways to Create Custom Tools\n\nLangChain provides three commonly used approaches:\n\n1. Using the `@tool` decorator.\n2. Using `StructuredTool` and Pydantic.\n3. Creating a custom class using `BaseTool`.\n\n### Method 1: Using the @tool Decorator\n\nThis is one of the simplest ways to create a custom tool.\n\n**Step 1: Create a normal Python function**\n\n```python\ndef multiply(a, b):\n    \"\"\"Multiply two numbers.\"\"\"\n    return a * b\n```\n\nAt this stage, it is just a normal Python function.\n\n**Step 2: Add type hints**\n\n```python\ndef multiply(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Multiply two numbers.\"\"\"\n    return a * b\n```\n\nType hints specify:\n- `a: int` means `a` is expected to be an integer.\n- `b: int` means `b` is expected to be an integer.\n- `-> int` means the function is expected to return an integer.\n\n**Step 3: Add the @tool decorator**\n\n```python\nfrom langchain.tools import tool\n\n@tool\ndef multiply(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Multiply two numbers.\"\"\"\n    return a * b\n```\n\nThe `@tool` decorator converts the Python function into a LangChain tool.\n\n**Step 4: Invoke the tool**\n\n```python\nresult = multiply.invoke({\n    \"a\": 3,\n    \"b\": 5\n})\n\nprint(result)\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n15\n```\n\n**Understanding Tool Metadata**\n\nA tool contains metadata that helps an LLM understand its functionality.\n\n```python\nprint(multiply.name)\nprint(multiply.description)\nprint(multiply.args)\n```\n\nPossible output:\n\n```text\nmultiply\nMultiply two numbers\n{\n    'a': {'type': 'integer'},\n    'b': {'type': 'integer'}\n}\n```\n\nThe exact metadata representation can vary depending on the LangChain and Pydantic versions.\n\n### Method 2: Using StructuredTool and Pydantic\n\nA StructuredTool is a type of LangChain tool in which the input arguments follow a structured schema, typically defined using a Pydantic model.\n\nIt is useful when we want explicit control over the input structure, validation, and descriptions of arguments.\n\n**Step 1: Import the required classes**\n\n```python\nfrom langchain.tools import StructuredTool\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n```\n\n**Step 2: Define the input schema using Pydantic**\n\n```python\nclass MultiplyInput(BaseModel):\n\n    a: int = Field(\n        ...,\n        description=\"The first number to multiply\"\n    )\n\n    b: int = Field(\n        ...,\n        description=\"The second number to multiply\"\n    )\n```\n\nHere:\n\n- `BaseModel` is used to define a Pydantic model.\n- `a` and `b` are the expected inputs.\n- `int` specifies their data types.\n- `Field(...)` makes the field required.\n- `description` explains the purpose of each argument.\n\nThe Pydantic model defines the structure of the input expected by the tool.\n\n**Step 3: Create the actual Python function**\n\n```python\ndef multiply_func(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    return a * b\n```\n\n**Step 4: Create the StructuredTool**\n\n```python\nmultiply_tool = StructuredTool.from_function(\n    func=multiply_func,\n    name=\"multiply\",\n    description=\"Multiply two numbers\",\n    args_schema=MultiplyInput\n)\n```\n\nUnderstanding the parameters:\n\n| Parameter | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `func` | Python function that performs the operation. |\n| `name` | Name of the tool. |\n| `description` | Explains what the tool does. |\n| `args_schema` | Defines the expected input structure. |\n\n**Step 5: Invoke the tool**\n\n```python\nresult = multiply_tool.invoke({\n    \"a\": 3,\n    \"b\": 3\n})\n\nprint(result)\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n9\n```\n\n**Step 6: Access tool metadata**\n\n```python\nprint(multiply_tool.name)\n\nprint(multiply_tool.description)\n\nprint(multiply_tool.args)\n```\n\nPossible output:\n\n```text\nmultiply\n\nMultiply two numbers\n\n{\n    'a': {\n        'description': 'The first number to multiply',\n        'required': True,\n        'type': 'integer'\n    },\n    'b': {\n        'description': 'The second number to multiply',\n        'required': True,\n        'type': 'integer'\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe exact schema representation may vary between versions.\n\n### Method 3: Using BaseTool Class\n\n`BaseTool` is the abstract base class for tools in LangChain.\n\nIt defines the common structure and interface that tools must follow, whether they are simple tools or fully customized tools.\n\nOther tool implementations, including `@tool` and `StructuredTool`, are built on the BaseTool abstraction.\n\nWe can inherit from `BaseTool` to create a completely customized tool.\n\n**Step 1: Import the required classes**\n\n```python\nfrom langchain.tools import BaseTool\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\nfrom typing import Type\n```\n\n**Step 2: Define the input schema**\n\n```python\nclass MultiplyInput(BaseModel):\n\n    a: int = Field(\n        ...,\n        description=\"The first number to multiply\"\n    )\n\n    b: int = Field(\n        ...,\n        description=\"The second number to multiply\"\n    )\n```\n\n**Step 3: Create a custom tool class**\n\n```python\nclass MultiplyTool(BaseTool):\n\n    name: str = \"multiply\"\n\n    description: str = \"Multiply two numbers\"\n\n    args_schema: Type[BaseModel] = MultiplyInput\n\n    def _run(self, a: int, b: int) -> int:\n        return a * b\n```\n\nUnderstanding the important components:\n\n- `BaseTool`: The parent class from which our custom tool inherits.\n- `name`: The name of our tool.\n- `description`: Explains the functionality of the tool.\n- `args_schema`: Defines the expected arguments.\n- `_run()`: Contains the actual logic that executes when the tool is invoked synchronously.\n\n**Step 4: Create an instance**\n\n```python\nmultiply_tool = MultiplyTool()\n```\n\n**Step 5: Invoke the tool**\n\n```python\nresult = multiply_tool.invoke({\n    \"a\": 3,\n    \"b\": 3\n})\n\nprint(result)\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n9\n```\n\n**Step 6: Access metadata**\n\n```python\nprint(multiply_tool.name)\n\nprint(multiply_tool.description)\n\nprint(multiply_tool.args)\n```\n\nThe tool exposes its name, description, and input schema.\n\nNote: The exact implementation requirements of `BaseTool`, including asynchronous execution methods, can vary across LangChain versions. The example demonstrates the synchronous `_run()` implementation used in the lecture.\n\n## 6. Comparison of the Three Custom Tool Creation Methods\n\n| Feature | @tool | StructuredTool | BaseTool |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Approach | Decorator | Factory method | Class inheritance |\n| Input schema | Inferred from function | Explicit Pydantic schema | Explicit Pydantic schema |\n| Customization | Simple | More control over schema | Maximum control |\n| Code complexity | Low | Medium | Higher |\n| Best suited for | Simple functions | Structured inputs | Complex custom tools |\n\n**Which one should we use?**\n\n- Use `@tool` for simple Python functions.\n- Use `StructuredTool` when you want an explicitly defined input schema.\n- Use `BaseTool` when you need complete control over the tool's implementation and behaviour.\n\n## 7. Toolkits in LangChain\n\nA toolkit is a collection or bundle of related tools that serve a common purpose.\n\nInstead of managing individual tools separately, we can group related tools into one toolkit for convenience and reusability.\n\n### Example: Google Drive Toolkit\n\nA Google Drive toolkit might contain:\n\n| Tool | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `GoogleDriveCreateFileTool` | Upload or create a file. |\n| `GoogleDriveSearchTool` | Search for a file by name or content. |\n| `GoogleDriveReadFileTool` | Read the contents of a file. |\n\nAll these tools belong to the same toolkit because they are related to Google Drive operations.\n\n### Example: Creating a Custom Toolkit\n\nSuppose we want to create a MathToolkit containing two tools:\n\n- Addition\n- Multiplication\n\n**Step 1: Create the individual tools**\n\n```python\nfrom langchain.tools import tool\n\n@tool\ndef add(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Add two numbers.\"\"\"\n    return a + b\n\n\n@tool\ndef multiply(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Multiply two numbers.\"\"\"\n    return a * b\n```\n\n**Step 2: Create the toolkit class**\n\n```python\nclass MathToolkit:\n\n    def get_tools(self):\n        return [add, multiply]\n```\n\nHere, `get_tools()` returns a list containing all the tools belonging to the toolkit.\n\n**Step 3: Create an instance and retrieve the tools**\n\n```python\ntoolkit = MathToolkit()\n\ntools = toolkit.get_tools()\n```\n\n**Step 4: Iterate through the tools**\n\n```python\nfor tool in tools:\n    print(tool.name, \"=>\", tool.description)\n```\n\nPossible output:\n\n```text\nadd => Add two numbers.\nmultiply => Multiply two numbers.\n```\n\nNow we have a toolkit containing two related tools.\n\n**Important:** A toolkit is simply a way to organize and group related tools. It does not itself perform the operations; its individual tools do.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Quick Revision\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Tool | A function or API that an LLM can invoke to perform an action. |\n| Built-in Tool | A pre-built tool available through LangChain integrations. |\n| Custom Tool | A tool created by the developer. |\n| Tool Metadata | Information such as tool name, description, and arguments. |\n| Runnable | A common LangChain interface that provides methods like `invoke()`, `batch()`, and `stream()`. |\n| StructuredTool | A tool that uses a structured input schema, typically defined with Pydantic. |\n| BaseTool | Abstract base class providing the common interface for LangChain tools. |\n| Toolkit | A collection of related tools grouped together. |\n| Pydantic | A Python library used for data validation and schema definition. |\n| `@tool` | Decorator that converts a Python function into a LangChain tool. |\n| `args_schema` | Defines the expected input structure of a tool. |\n| `_run()` | Synchronous execution method in a custom BaseTool implementation. |\n\n## 9. Important Takeaways\n\n1. LLMs can reason and generate language, but external actions require tools or other integrations.\n2. Tools allow an agent to interact with external systems.\n3. Tools have a name, description, and input arguments.\n4. Built-in tools are already implemented; custom tools are created by developers.\n5. Tools follow the Runnable interface and support methods such as `invoke()`.\n6. We can create custom tools using `@tool`, `StructuredTool`, or `BaseTool`.\n7. Pydantic helps define and validate structured inputs.\n8. A toolkit is a collection of related tools.\n\n**The most important distinction:**\n\nA Tool performs an individual operation, whereas a Toolkit groups multiple related tools together.\n\nAn Agent uses the LLM's decision-making capabilities to decide which available tool should be called to accomplish a task.","metadata":{}}]}